.docname {Releasing}
.include {docs}

Releases are built from tags. CI builds Linux, Windows, and macOS and publishes them as a GitHub release.

## Keep the changelog

`CHANGELOG.md` lists the changes in each release, written for people who use the app rather than copied from commit messages. When a pull request changes something a user would notice, it adds a line under `## [Unreleased]`, in the section that fits: **Added**, **Changed**, **Fixed**, or **Removed**. Refactors, tests, and CI changes don't need one.

## Bump the version

In one commit:

1. Rename `## [Unreleased]` to the new version and today's date, such as `## [0.2.0] - 2026-12-01`, and start an empty `## [Unreleased]` above it.
2. Update the links at the bottom of the file: `[Unreleased]` now compares from the new tag, and the new version links to its release.
3. Set the new version in `Cargo.toml`, then run `cargo build` so `Cargo.lock` picks it up.

```shell
cargo build
git commit -am "release 0.2.0"
```

CI refuses a tag that doesn't match this version, so the tag, the release, and the crate always name the same version. Check the tag before pushing it:

```shell
just release-check v0.2.0
```

This runs `scripts/check-release-version.sh`, which passes only if the tag is `v` followed by the version in `Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock` has the same version, and `CHANGELOG.md` has notes under that version.

## Tag the release

```shell
git tag v0.2.0 && git push origin v0.2.0
```

On a tag, CI runs the usual lint and test jobs, then:

| Job               | Output                                         | Notes                                    |
|-------------------|------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|
| `version`         | Nothing                                        | Runs the same check as `just release-check`; no release job starts unless it passes |
| `release-linux`   | `gol-bevy-<tag>-x86_64-linux.tar.gz`           | Built on Debian trixie, so it needs glibc 2.39 or newer |
| `release-windows` | `gol-bevy-<tag>-x86_64-windows.zip`            | Cross-compiled with MinGW, so no Windows runner is needed |
| `release-macos`   | `gol-bevy-<tag>-universal-macos.tar.gz`        | Universal binary for Apple silicon and Intel, built on a macOS runner |
| `release`         | A GitHub release named `gol-bevy <tag>`        | Has all three archives attached, and the version's notes from `CHANGELOG.md` |

## Release notes

The release job takes the notes from `CHANGELOG.md` with `scripts/release-notes.sh`: a line on how to run the download, then the version's section, without its heading. To preview them:

```shell
scripts/release-notes.sh 0.2.0
```

Edit the notes on the release page afterwards to add anything more, and fix the changelog to match.

## Building for macOS locally

`release-macos` runs the same recipe CI uses. On a Mac with `cargo-about` installed (see [Licence notices](#licence-notices)), run it to try a release build:

```shell
just release-macos v0.2.0
```

This adds both Apple targets, builds for Apple silicon and Intel, joins them into a universal binary with `lipo`, and writes `dist/gol-bevy-v0.2.0-universal-macos.tar.gz`. Without a version, it uses `git describe --tags --always`.

## Licence notices

Every archive and the web version carry `THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.html`, the licences of every crate compiled in, on every platform gol-bevy is built for. `scripts/third-party-licenses.sh` writes it with [cargo-about](https://github.com/EmbarkStudios/cargo-about), from `about.toml` and the `about.hbs` template:

```shell
cargo install cargo-about --locked --features cli
scripts/third-party-licenses.sh    # writes dist/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.html
```

`about.toml` lists the licences dependencies may use. A dependency under any other licence fails the release build; check that its licence allows shipping it in a binary under MIT, then add it to the list.

## Archive layout

`scripts/package-release.sh` packages every platform the same way:

```shell
scripts/package-release.sh <archive name> <binary> [tar.gz|zip]
```

It copies the binary, `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `LICENSE`, and the font's `assets/fonts/OFL.txt` into a folder named after the archive, writes `THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.html` beside them, packs it into `dist/`, and prints the archive path.

.filetree
    - dist
      - gol-bevy-v0.2.0-x86_64-linux.tar.gz
        - gol-bevy-v0.2.0-x86_64-linux
          - gol-bevy
          - README.md
          - CHANGELOG.md
          - LICENSE
          - OFL.txt
          - THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.html
